From: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: sunxi: run the H713 ARISC from Linux and give it a HDMI API

The H713 carries an OpenRISC core next to the ARM cluster -- ARISC, AR100,
"cpus" in the vendor sources -- and it owns the R_ peripherals. Among those
are the HDMI hotplug pin, the EDID store and the DDC RAM, so nothing about
this SoC's HDMI input works until firmware is running on that core. On a
vendor system the secure monitor loads it; mainline TF-A does not, and up to
now the gap was papered over from userspace: a Python loader wrote the image
through /dev/mem and a shell script played the EDID sequence.

This driver takes both jobs. request_firmware() fetches h713-arisc.bin, the
image goes into SRAM A2, the core is released, and the startup handshake the
firmware waits for is completed before anything else happens. The HDMI
sub-commands become an in-kernel interface in
include/linux/soc/sunxi/h713-arisc.h for the V4L2 receive driver to build
VIDIOC_S_EDID/G_EDID and hotplug handling on, and the whole stock EDID
sequence can be triggered from debugfs so a bring-up needs no userspace
helper at all.

It replaces the earlier debugfs-only hotplug module. That one carried three
things that turned out to be symptoms of one cause, and all three are gone
here: a doorbell pulse on TX_IRQ_EN, a "main loop alive" probe, and an
"unstick" that pushed zero words at the standby dispatcher to free a wedged
main loop. The cause is the startup handshake below -- unstick worked because
its zero words happened to be exactly the acknowledgement the firmware was
waiting for.

Loading follows the vendor monitor's sunxi_arisc_probe, confirmed against the
vendor 3.4 kernel driver and against crust: enable the three R_PRCM CPUS
timer gates that boot0 turns on, hold the core in reset (R_CPUCFG bit 0),
copy the first 140 KiB of the image to 0x00100000, write struct arisc_para,
release the core. The image is copied a word at a time, because a wide or
unaligned store into that window faults, and it is copied verbatim: the
AR100's data bus swaps every 32-bit word and the vendor image is stored
pre-swapped, so byte-identical is correct and swapping again would destroy
the firmware silently. It is read back afterwards -- strictly from SRAM A2
on, and in the vector area only on the 0x100 boundaries, since the hardware
accepts one word per boundary there.

The vendor also copies a 32 KiB tail of the image to DRAM 0x48100000. That
address is inside kernel text on this memory map, and the firmware has come
up without the tail across every cold start we have measured, so the tail is
not copied. If a service ever needs it, it needs a reserved-memory region
first; the devicetree comment says so.

The startup handshake is the reason this driver exists in the shape it does.
After the reset is released the firmware pushes a notify onto ARM-RX channel
3 -- header, count, then its version string -- and then waits for the ARM to
answer on user1 port 3. Until that answer arrives it sits in front of its
main loop and serves no HDMI command, and without a reader the notify itself
stalls at word 9 because the FIFO is eight deep. Measured: no reader, ~100 s
of nothing; reader but no answer, ~100 s; answer, first command served 0.2 s
later. Only a notify is answered, and only one with result 0 and a non-zero
count: replying to anything else -- for instance the standby dispatcher's own
"unknown type" answer -- starts a ping-pong at 100 ms that never stops.

Nothing knocks. The firmware's main loop polls the FIFO status of user1
port 0 and of port 3, and the startup waiter polls port 3 as well, so the
TX_IRQ_EN pulse the old module used had nothing to wake.

A sub-command goes in through the receive pump on user1 port 0, and its shape
is dictated by one quirk: the pump keeps the frame length in a register
rather than in its context, and its poller wakes it as soon as the first word
lands, so a frame that arrives in two pieces loses its length. The pump's
classifier reads the sub-command out of its payload buffer instead, so the
payload is written into that buffer and the frame carries length 0. That is
immune to the race rather than hoping to win it.

Every command is confirmed, and every wait ends in an error rather than in
optimism:

  - the 0x11 group copies 65 bytes of the payload into its scratch area
    before dispatching, so the driver primes that area with the complement
    of the payload and waits for it to match. That is exact for every
    command in the group, including the EDID fragments; the predecessor
    counted zeroed words, which only worked while the payload was zeros.
  - the 0x15 group answers with a frame on ARM-RX channel 1, and that frame
    carries a header the reader has to skip. The framed sender 0x118e4 wraps
    the payload (0xff in front, a checksum at [len - 2], 0xfe at [len - 1])
    and hands it to 0x81c8, which pushes eight header bytes of its own first:
    a5 | seq << 8 | type << 16 | length << 24, then a word of zeros. That is
    the same BOP header this driver sends on user1 port 0, so the payload
    starts at word 2 and is <length> bytes long. CheckEDIDUpdateStatus
    answers ff f8 01 01 <ready>, built at 0x125a0..0x125c0, and <ready> is
    [0x1723e] -- the firmware's own g_Data.bEdidDataReady, cleared by every
    UpdateEDID fragment 0..6 and set by fragment 7 before it publishes the
    store. It is therefore the answer to the question the command asks, and
    the driver fails the upload without it. RequestEDID uses the same sender
    and replies with four such frames, one per 64-byte fragment of the block.
  - PullHotPlug is checked against the firmware's own two-stage counter: a
    ~100 Hz timer walks it down to 1 and only the main loop takes it to 0 and
    raises the pin, so the counter reaching 0 is proof the pin moved, and a
    RESET has to pass through ~84 on the way. The counter alone is not proof
    of a rising edge, though: the routine that drives the pin, 0x121e4, reads
    [0x1723f] first and, while that byte is clear, prints "EDID-HPD %d LOW"
    and overwrites its own value argument with 0 -- the pin stays low while
    the main loop still walks the counter down. [0x1723f] is set at the very
    end of the publish routine 0x11d88, right after "edid updated", so a
    clear byte means no EDID was ever published and there is nothing for a
    sink to read. UP and RESET refuse rather than report a rise that did not
    happen.

EDID upload sends both versions in one go, fragments 0..3 for the HDMI 1.4
block and 4..7 for the HDMI 2.0 block, because the firmware only publishes an
EDID once fragment 7 has arrived: every earlier fragment clears the data-ready
byte and only the last one sets it and calls the output routine. That is also
why arisc_hdmi_set_edid() takes no port -- the store is global.

One ordering rule is enforced rather than documented. Reading anything in
0x0709xxxx from the ARM before the firmware has processed ResetEDIDModule
hangs the SoC hard: the block has no clock yet, and it took three dead boards
to establish that. This driver maps no register in that range, and every
entry point except arisc_hdmi_reset_edid() fails with -EAGAIN until that
command has been acknowledged, so a consumer cannot reach the aux block
through this interface out of order.

Devicetree binding (this tree carries no YAML schemas, so it is described
here and in the node's comment):

  compatible	"allwinner,sun50i-h713-arisc"
  reg		five windows, named through reg-names:
		  "sram"	SRAM A2 as the ARM sees it, 0x00100000 +
				0x23000. The image goes to the start of it;
				the running firmware keeps its message
				buffer, HDMI port state and hotplug counters
				in the same window.
		  "msgbox-rx"	msgbox bank user0, first sub-block: the
				channels the ARISC answers on.
		  "msgbox-tx"	msgbox bank user1, first sub-block: port 0
				is the HDMI receive pump, port 3 the standby
				dispatcher.
		  "reset"	R_CPUCFG; bit 0 holds or runs the core.
		  "cpus-clk"	the three R_PRCM gates boot0 enables.
  firmware-name	index 0 the ARISC image, index 1 the default EDID (both
		blocks, 512 bytes).

Two of those windows overlap a block another node describes, and the driver
therefore maps them without claiming the region: the msgbox banks lie inside
cpu_comm@3003000, which uses +0x164/+0x174 and +0x864/+0x874 and shares no
register with this driver, and the CPUS clock gates lie inside the r_ccu
register file, whose D1 model starts at +0x11c and does not describe them.

What is measured on a HY310 projector with firmware TV-303 00.00.00.09 is the
sequence itself and every rule above: the loader sequence, the startup
handshake and its timings, the payload-buffer trick, the fragment ordering,
the status reply, the hotplug counter, and the aux-block hang.

The bring-up run on 07.09.2026 confirmed the loader, the reset release and
the startup handshake -- including that no doorbell pulse is needed -- and
then walked the EDID sequence through ResetEDIDModule, HostHDMIMAP,
SetEDIDVersion and all eight fragments. It stopped at the status check with

    Antwort auf 0x0215: 4 Woerter, 2 uebernommen
    CheckEDIDUpdateStatus antwortet a5 00 01 08 00 00 00 00 statt ff f8 01 01

and that line is the frame header, not the payload: a5 is the marker, 00 the
sequence number of the first framed reply after a fresh start, 01 the type
0x81c8 stamps on, 08 the payload length, and the zero word is the header's
mandatory second word. The eight payload bytes were in words 2 and 3 and were
dropped, because the status buffer is eight bytes wide and the old reader
counted the header into it -- the same run's "4 Woerter" is exactly the two
header words plus the two payload words. Nothing about that measurement says
the firmware refused the EDID; the driver simply never looked at the answer.
Reading the frame is what this revision adds. The steps after the status
check have not run yet.

Board run 07.09.2026, 06:45: the sequence runs to the end and returns 0 --
notify acked, EDID uploaded, status ff f8 01 01 01 00 07 fe, publish gate
set, EDID read back -- and the sink still says disconnected, while the same
commands with the same arguments and the same order from the userspace
script leave it connected. The aux register the hotplug pin sits in ended at
0x03 instead of the 0x06 a stock run shows: the pulse moved bit 2, not bit
0. Bit 2 is what the pin driver picks when the port map says port 0 has pin
2, and the map the firmware ended up with is the payload of the *next*
command. HostHDMIMAP is the only sub-command of the 0x11 group that reads
its arguments out of the receive pump's buffer after the memset this driver
waits for, and it does so on the far side of a log call. The script's 600 ms
between sub-commands hides that; this driver's next payload lands about a
millisecond later. So arisc_hdmi_set_portmap() waits for the port map
records themselves and fails loudly when they do not carry the pins that
were asked for.

That wait only means something if it can fail, and its first form could not.
The map the stock sequence asks for is 0,1,2, and 0,1,2 is what the records
already contain: the firmware's own init copies them out of a ROM table at
0x15edc -- 01 10 00 01 | 02 20 01 02 | 04 30 02 04, i.e. mask, tag, pin,
1 << pin -- and nothing but HostHDMIMAP writes them afterwards. A wait for
"the records carry 0,1,2" therefore returned in its first pass on every
freshly started firmware and reported success whether the handler had run or
not; the board run on 07.09.2026 08:27 duly showed portmap: 0,1,2, which
proved nothing. So the two bytes are primed with 0xff before the command
goes out, a value no pin (0..2) and no 1 << pin (1, 2, 4) can be and no
payload of the next command can leave behind, and the priming is read back
before anything is sent -- if it did not land the command is not sent at all,
because a check that cannot fail is worse than no check. A match is then
only reachable through a firmware write, a timeout is a real failure, and the
two failures read differently: priming still in place means the handler never
reached its stores, a plausible but wrong pin means it reached them after the
buffer had been overwritten. On failure the records are put back the way they
were found, so a failed command leaves the firmware as it was.

0xff is safe to leave in those two bytes for the ~1 ms the handler needs, and
this is the argument, not an assumption: every reader of them is in the
image. Byte 3 is read only from the publish routine and the module reset,
both of which run under a sub-command and are serialised by the driver's own
mutex -- and 0x11cac cannot even tell the difference, it ORs the byte in and
tests bit 0. Byte 2 is read by the pin driver 0x121e4, whose dispatch knows
0, 1 and 2 and sends everything else to 0x12304, which writes the aux
register back with the value it just read: a pulse landing inside the window
would be dropped, never misdirected. The one path that can reach 0x121e4
without a command of ours is the main loop walking a hotplug counter down to
1, so the command is refused outright while any counter is non-zero. Byte 4
is left alone because the reverse lookup at 0x11c64 walks it, and the same
three stores write it.

debugfs status still prints the map the firmware holds, with a comment saying
what it is worth: it is state, not a verdict -- 0,1,2 there means nothing on
its own, but anything else, 2,0,0 above all, names the payload the firmware
read instead.

Signed-off-by: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff -ruN a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi	2026-09-06 22:02:26.614214570 +0200
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi	2026-09-06 22:02:47.521528457 +0200
@@ -1441,6 +1441,63 @@
 		};
 
 		/*
+		 * ARISC co-processor (OpenRISC "AR100", "cpus") -- OURS, not in
+		 * cstenger's tree. It owns the R_ peripherals, among them the
+		 * HDMI hotplug pin and the EDID/DDC block, so the HDMI input
+		 * cannot come up without firmware running on it. The vendor
+		 * secure monitor loads that firmware; mainline TF-A does not, so
+		 * sun50i-h713-arisc does it from Linux.
+		 *
+		 * Binding:
+		 *   compatible    "allwinner,sun50i-h713-arisc"
+		 *   reg/reg-names five windows, in this order:
+		 *     "sram"       SRAM A2 as the ARM sees it. The firmware image
+		 *                  goes to the start of it (140 KiB); the running
+		 *                  firmware keeps its message buffer, its HDMI
+		 *                  port state and its hotplug counters in the
+		 *                  same window.
+		 *     "msgbox-rx"  msgbox bank user0, first sub-block: the
+		 *                  channels the ARISC answers on (startup notify
+		 *                  on ch3, EDID and status on ch1).
+		 *     "msgbox-tx"  msgbox bank user1, first sub-block: port 0
+		 *                  reaches the HDMI receive pump, port 3 the
+		 *                  standby dispatcher that wants the startup
+		 *                  acknowledgement.
+		 *     "reset"      R_CPUCFG. Bit 0 holds (0) or runs (1) the core.
+		 *     "cpus-clk"   the three R_PRCM gates boot0 turns on before
+		 *                  the image is copied ("prcm cpus timer clock
+		 *                  enable").
+		 *   firmware-name index 0 the ARISC image, index 1 the default
+		 *                 EDID (both blocks, 512 bytes) the driver hands
+		 *                 to the firmware.
+		 *
+		 * Two of the windows deliberately overlap a block another node
+		 * already describes, so the driver maps them without claiming the
+		 * region: the msgbox banks sit inside cpu_comm@3003000 (which
+		 * uses +0x164/+0x174 and +0x864/+0x874 -- no register is shared),
+		 * and the CPUS clock gates sit inside the r_ccu register file
+		 * (whose D1 model starts at +0x11c and does not describe them).
+		 *
+		 * The vendor loader also copies a 32 KiB tail of the image to
+		 * DRAM 0x48100000, which is kernel text here. The firmware runs
+		 * without it, so there is no reserved-memory region for it; if a
+		 * service ever turns out to need the tail, it needs a carveout
+		 * first.
+		 */
+		arisc: arisc@100000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h713-arisc";
+			reg = <0x00100000 0x23000>,
+			      <0x03003000 0x100>,
+			      <0x03003400 0x100>,
+			      <0x07000400 0x4>,
+			      <0x07010110 0xc>;
+			reg-names = "sram", "msgbox-rx", "msgbox-tx",
+				    "reset", "cpus-clk";
+			firmware-name = "h713-arisc.bin", "hy310-edid.bin";
+			status = "okay";
+		};
+
+		/*
 		 * H713 PPU — power domain controller.
 		 *
 		 * Source is now the STOCK DTB (local/stock-boot/sunxi.fex), which
diff -ruN a/drivers/soc/sunxi/Kconfig b/drivers/soc/sunxi/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/Kconfig	2026-09-06 22:02:26.611547783 +0200
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/Kconfig	2026-09-06 22:05:34.027398447 +0200
@@ -21,3 +21,21 @@
 	  whether to the CPU/DMA, or to the devices.
 
 source "drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/Kconfig"
